   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the county law and the municipal home rule law, in relation to\nwebsites maintained by a county",
    "basePrintNo": "S8561",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8561-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §55, County L; add §35-a, Munic Home R L",
    "lawSection": "County Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8561\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the county law and the municipal home rule law, in\nrelation to websites maintained by a county\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends the county law by requiring any county that maintains\na website to use a .gov domain for such website\n\nSection 2: amends the municipal home rule law by requiring any chartered\ncounty that maintains a website to use a .gov domain for such website.\n\nSection 3: provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIt is imperative that New Yorkers can confidently access accurate\ngovernmental information when accessing a county website. Currently,\ncounties do not need to maintain a .gov domain for their websites.\nThese websites often use .org or .com domains, which can easily be used\nor set up by cyber attackers as a way to misinform the public.\n\nA person could feasibly buy a .org or .com domain name and create a fake\nwebsite to target an area or population. These falsified websites could\nlook identical to the county website, but with minor changes. Unlike\n.org or .com domains, websites that maintain or request a .gov domain\nare approved and monitored by the federal government.\n\nThere is currently not a way for the public to discern between a credi-\nble county website and a falsified website with incorrect information.\nRequiring all county websites to use a .gov domain is a simple, yet\neffective way to prevent the rapid spread of misinformation. This way,\nindividuals will be able to distinguish between the real county sites\nand any fake ones.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect one year after it shall have become a law.\n",
